@Andrzej

Sportscasters say "pick" as a synonym for "interception" because its meaning is self-evident; announcers just as easily say that the ball was picked off, using good old Anglo-Saxon words instead of Latin ones, and of course you know Americans' penchant for shortening things whenever possible.

PICK-SIX, however, has another element of cleverness to it.  In American lotteries, you have to pick a certain number of numbers.  Often, that number is six.  So those lottery tickets were called PICK-SIX; others where called PICK-FIVE, etc.  So the appellation of PICK-SIX to interceptions that are returned for a touchdown is a clever play on the lottery ticket meaning.

